---
// EcosystemSection — the #ecosystem section body: a short narrative explaining how
// the projects in the Work grid reinforce each other as a system of gated,
// self-improving loops (a build pipeline that improves itself, a control plane
// that watches every project, instruments that keep the loops honest, and the
// job-search loop that feeds this very résumé). Every factual claim traces to the
// named repo's own README/plan — no invented metrics. Projects are referenced by
// name (they carry their own links in the Work grid above), so this section adds
// no external links of its own. Owns its <section> (id + aria-labelledby +
// sticky-header scroll offset); all colors/spacing/type resolve from the brand
// semantic tokens, so it reads intentionally in BOTH light and dark.
import { ANCHORS } from "../consts";

// One self-reinforcing loop per entry: a short term + the paragraph that grounds
// it. Rendered as a <dl> so assistive tech announces term → description pairs.
const LOOPS = [
  {
    term: "The pipeline that builds the projects",
    body: "Most of what's on this site was built with skill-mesh — a provider-neutral library of ~50 AI-agent skills that runs agent work as a gated plan → build → review → ship pipeline. One shared behavior contract per skill drives two hosts, Claude Code and GitHub Copilot, from a single install. The same library improves itself: it compiles a skill's own spec into a machine-scorable eval suite, hill-climbs skills overnight, and A/B-tests variant rewrites in parallel, so the tooling behind the projects is under continuous, measured revision. Alpha4Gate and void-furnace are what that pipeline produced — and each embeds its own smaller self-improvement loop.",
  },
  {
    term: "The watcher",
    body: "A control plane, dev-observatory, watches every project in the workspace — 31 registered repos — entirely from the outside. It reads the descriptor files each project already emits, derives what's shipped versus planned and how to launch each one, and never writes into a tree it doesn't own: an explicit ownership flag hard-blocks it. Nothing on this list depends on it; it only observes.",
  },
  {
    term: "The instruments that keep the loops honest",
    body: "Three tools enforce one shared rule — no system grades its own work. measure-twice turns “which model should do which job” into a measurement, with every scorer gated forever against frozen good-and-garbage anchors. switchboard offloads the cheap judgment calls inside those pipelines to a local model, but never lets it sit on a correctness gate. citation-needed puts a verified citation trail under the design choices baked into all of it, and makes fabricated citations structurally impossible.",
  },
  {
    term: "The loop that closes it",
    body: "The job search itself runs on career-ops — an open-source pipeline by santifer that I run and customize, not one I built. It scores roles against my CV and renders tailored, ATS-safe PDFs; the résumé you can download from this page is generated by it. The projects feed the CV, the CV feeds the applications, and what comes back sharpens the targeting — the last loop in the system, and the one that ties it back to this site.",
  },
];
---

<section
  id={ANCHORS.ecosystem}
  class="ecosystem-section"
  aria-labelledby="ecosystem-heading"
>
  <h2 id="ecosystem-heading">How it all fits together</h2>
  <p class="ecosystem-lede">
    The projects above aren't separate experiments — they're one system of
    self-reinforcing loops, built and watched with a shared discipline: anything
    that improves itself has to prove it through a gate it can't pass by luck,
    and no loop grades its own work. Here's how the pieces feed each other.
  </p>
  <dl class="ecosystem-loops">
    {
      LOOPS.map((loop) => (
        <div class="ecosystem-loop">
          <dt>{loop.term}</dt>
          <dd>{loop.body}</dd>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </dl>
</section>

<style>
  .ecosystem-section {
    max-width: 48rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: var(--space-8) var(--space-4);
    /* Offset the sticky header so the anchored heading isn't hidden under it. */
    scroll-margin-top: 5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-semantic-border);
  }

  .ecosystem-section h2 {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-4);
    color: var(--color-semantic-text);
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-size: var(--type-scale-step-3);
    font-weight: var(--type-weight-bold);
    line-height: var(--type-line-height-tight);
  }

  .ecosystem-lede {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-6);
    max-width: 42rem;
    color: var(--color-semantic-text-muted);
    font-size: var(--type-scale-step-0);
    line-height: var(--type-line-height-base);
  }

  .ecosystem-loops {
    margin: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-5);
  }

  .ecosystem-loop {
    /* An accent rule on the leading edge ties the four loops together visually
     * without a heavy card treatment; logical padding keeps it correct in RTL. */
    padding-inline-start: var(--space-4);
    border-inline-start: 2px solid var(--color-semantic-accent);
  }

  .ecosystem-loop dt {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2);
    color: var(--color-semantic-text);
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-size: var(--type-scale-step-1);
    font-weight: var(--type-weight-semibold);
    line-height: var(--type-line-height-tight);
  }

  .ecosystem-loop dd {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 42rem;
    color: var(--color-semantic-text);
    font-size: var(--type-scale-step-0);
    line-height: var(--type-line-height-base);
  }
</style>
